.TH "FDS" "1" "" "fds 0.0.7" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fds \- search the string values of Arrow and Parquet files
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]fds\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]] \f[I]path\f[R] \f[I]term\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]fds\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]] \f[B]\-e\f[R] \f[I]term\f[R]
[\f[B]\-e\f[R] \f[I]term\f[R]]\&...
\f[I]path\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]fds\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]] \f[B]\-f\f[R] \f[I]file\f[R]
\f[I]path\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]fds help\f[R] [\f[I]topic\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]fds\f[R] searches every string value of every Arrow IPC file
(\f[I].arrow\f[R], \f[I].feather\f[R], \f[I].arrows\f[R]) and Parquet
file (\f[I].parquet\f[R]) under \f[I]path\f[R], or of \f[I]path\f[R]
itself if it is a file (whatever its name), and prints
\f[I]path\f[R]:\f[I]column\f[R]:\f[I]row\f[R] for each value that
contains \f[I]term\f[R].
.PP
Rows are 0\-based and count from the start of the file.
The column is the value\(cqs place in its row: \f[I]user.name\f[R] for a
struct field, \f[I]tags[1]\f[R] for element 1 of a list,
\f[I]m[key]\f[R] for the value of a map\(cqs entry \f[I]key\f[R] and
\f[I]m[key].key\f[R] for its key.
Each matching value is a line.
Options that take a column (\f[B]\-c\f[R]) and \f[B]\-\-count\f[R] use
column paths, with positions left out: \f[I]tags[]\f[R],
\f[I]m[].value\f[R], \f[I]m[].key\f[R].
Files come out in no fixed order; within a file, matches are ordered by
row group (or record batch), column, then row.
.PP
Strings fds can\(cqt search (in Arrow unions, run\-end\-encoded arrays
and list views) and files it can\(cqt read are reported on standard
error.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Matching
.TP
\f[B]\-i\f[R], \f[B]\-\-ignore\-case\f[R]
Match ASCII letters regardless of case.
.TP
\f[B]\-S\f[R], \f[B]\-\-smart\-case\f[R]
Ignore case unless a term has an upper\-case letter.
.TP
\f[B]\-E\f[R], \f[B]\-\-regex\f[R]
Terms are regular expressions: \f[B].
[a\-z] [\(ha0\-9] \(rsd \(rsw \(rss \(rsb \(ha $ * + ?
{n,m} | ( )\f[R] and \f[B]\(rsn \(rsr \(rst\f[R].
\f[B]\(ha\f[R] and \f[B]$\f[R] match at the start and end of the value.
Built with PCRE2 (\f[B]zig build \-Dpcre2\f[R]; see \f[B]\-V\f[R]), the
full PCRE2 syntax.
.TP
\f[B]\-w\f[R], \f[B]\-\-word\f[R]
Match whole words only.
.TP
\f[B]\-e\f[R], \f[B]\-\-regexp\f[R] \f[I]term\f[R]
A term to search for; repeat it to match any of several.
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\-\-file\f[R] \f[I]file\f[R]
Terms, one per line (\f[B]\-\f[R] for standard input); empty lines are
ignored.
From 8 terms on, they are searched together in one pass, so thousands
are fine.
Repeatable, and can be mixed with \f[B]\-e\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-c\f[R], \f[B]\-\-column\f[R] \f[I]name\f[R]
Search only this column: a top\-level name (everything under it), or a
path such as \f[I]user.name\f[R] or \f[I]tags[]\f[R].
Repeatable.
.SS Output
.TP
\f[B]\-\-content\f[R]
Also print the value:
\f[I]path\f[R]:\f[I]column\f[R]:\f[I]row\f[R]:\f[I]value\f[R].
Newlines in it are printed as \f[B]\(rsn\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-l\f[R], \f[B]\-\-files\-with\-matches\f[R]
Print only the files that match; each file\(cqs search stops at its
first match.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-count\f[R]
Print \f[I]path\f[R]:\f[I]column\f[R]:\f[I]count\f[R] for each column
with matches.
.TP
\f[B]\-m\f[R], \f[B]\-\-max\-count\f[R] \f[I]n\f[R]
Stop after \f[I]n\f[R] matches in each file.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-json\f[R]
JSON Lines:
\f[B]{\(lqpath\(rq,\(lqcolumn\(rq,\(lqat\(rq,\(lqrow\(rq,\(lqvalue\(rq}\f[R],
\f[I]column\f[R] being the column path and \f[I]at\f[R] the place, or
\f[B]{\(lqpath\(rq}\f[R] with \f[B]\-l\f[R] and
\f[B]{\(lqpath\(rq,\(lqcolumn\(rq,\(lqcount\(rq}\f[R] with
\f[B]\-\-count\f[R].
Bytes that aren\(cqt UTF\-8 are given base64\-encoded, as
\f[B]value_base64\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-color\f[R] \f[I]when\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] (the default: on a terminal), \f[B]always\f[R] or
\f[B]never\f[R].
.SS Other
.TP
\f[B]\-j\f[R] \f[I]n\f[R]|\f[B]auto\f[R]
Worker threads.
\f[B]auto\f[R], the default, is every CPU, or the performance cores on
Apple silicon.
.TP
\f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Print the version, and the regex engine if it is PCRE2.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Print a summary of the options.
.SH TOPICS
\f[B]fds help\f[R] lists the topics, and \f[B]fds help\f[R]
\f[I]topic\f[R] prints one: \f[B]search\f[R], \f[B]matching\f[R],
\f[B]many\-terms\f[R], \f[B]columns\f[R], \f[B]output\f[R],
\f[B]scripting\f[R], \f[B]formats\f[R].
They are the pages of the documentation, with examples and their output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Something matched.
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
Nothing matched.
.TP
\f[B]2\f[R]
An error: a file couldn\(cqt be read, or strings couldn\(cqt be
searched, even if something matched elsewhere.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]NO_COLOR\f[R]
If set, no colors with \f[B]\-\-color auto\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
Every match of a term under a directory:
.IP
.EX
fds data/ \(aqhost\-4242.example\(aq
.EE
.PP
One column, with the values; any case, whole words:
.IP
.EX
fds \-\-content \-c user.name \-i \-w data/events.arrow needle
.EE
.PP
A regex, with matches counted per column:
.IP
.EX
fds \-E \-\-count data/ \(aq\(ha/api/v[0\-9]+/checkout/\(rsd+$\(aq
.EE
.PP
The files that hold any of 10,000 terms:
.IP
.EX
fds \-l \-f questions.txt train/
.EE
.PP
JSON Lines, for \f[B]jq\f[R] or pandas:
.IP
.EX
fds \-\-json data/ needle | jq .row
.EE
